{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-274686",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)+2 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) & =-2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =\\pi +2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{\\pi +2n\\pi }{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(2n+1)\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Setting $n=4$ gives $x=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{5},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{5},\\,{x}_{3}=\\pi ,\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{7\\pi }{5},\\,{x}_{5}=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
